        "excerpt": "Preeclampsia can develop during pregnancy or after birth, sometimes without obvious symptoms. Know the warning signs and when to get urgent care.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "Yes. High blood pressure and protein in urine may be found at routine appointments before you notice symptoms. This is one reason regular prenatal checks are important.",
            "question": "Can you have preeclampsia without symptoms?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "It usually develops after 20 weeks of pregnancy, often later in pregnancy, but it can also begin after childbirth. Symptoms during the postpartum weeks still need urgent assessment.",
            "question": "When does preeclampsia usually start?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Gradual swelling can be common in pregnancy, but sudden swelling—especially of the face or hands—can be a warning sign when combined with other symptoms. Seek prompt advice rather than judging swelling alone.",
            "question": "Is swelling always a sign of preeclampsia?"
          }
        ],
