        "excerpt": "A realistic guide to physical and emotional recovery during the first six weeks after vaginal or caesarean birth, including symptoms that need urgent help.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "Lochia usually becomes lighter and changes colour over several weeks. NHS guidance says it often stops by six to eight weeks, but it can last longer. Sudden heavy bleeding, faintness, fever, worsening pain or an unusual smell needs prompt assessment.",
            "question": "How long does postpartum bleeding last?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Timing varies. ACOG recommends contact within three weeks and a comprehensive visit by 12 weeks; the NHS offers a postnatal check at six to eight weeks. High-risk symptoms or concerns require earlier care.",
            "question": "When should I have a postpartum check?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Yes. A caesarean adds abdominal surgery and wound care, and some activities may be uncomfortable for six weeks or longer. You will still have vaginal lochia and need monitoring for infection, clots and heavy bleeding.",
            "question": "Is recovery different after a caesarean birth?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": false,
