        "excerpt": "A practical guide to common pregnancy scans, what they can reveal and why your personal ultrasound schedule may look different.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "There is no universal number. England routinely offers two, while ACOG describes at least one standard scan, usually at 18 to 22 weeks. Your country, health and pregnancy findings determine your schedule.",
            "question": "How many ultrasounds do you need during pregnancy?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "No. Ultrasound can screen for many structural conditions and assess growth, but it cannot detect every condition and some views may be limited by gestation or baby position.",
            "question": "Can an ultrasound detect every condition?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Diagnostic ultrasound has no known harmful effects when used appropriately. It should be performed for a medical reason by qualified professionals, with the lowest exposure needed to obtain useful information.",
            "question": "Are extra scans safe?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": false,
        "heading": null,
        "heroImage": {
          "src": "https://cdn.sanity.io/images/hphu8zlu/production/223bbad4f8471dfc5369eae336e006cbf99707a2-1672x941.png?w=1600&fit=max&auto=format",
          "alt": "Pregnant patient viewing an ultrasound image with a healthcare professional",
          "width": 1672,
          "height": 941,
          "credit": "HelloMommy original editorial image"
        },
        "intro": "Ultrasound uses sound waves to create images of your baby, placenta and pregnancy. The exact pregnancy ultrasound schedule depends on where you receive care, your medical history and how the pregnancy is progressing. In England, routine care usually includes scans at 11 to 14 weeks and 18 to 21 weeks; ACOG says at least one standard scan is generally performed, usually at 18 to 22 weeks. Extra scans are not automatically a sign that something is wrong.",
        "locale": "en",
        "publishedAt": "2026-08-28",
