        "excerpt": "Learn when you may first feel your baby move, how movements change, what is normal for your baby and when a change needs urgent assessment.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "It is unusual to recognise fetal movement at 12 weeks. Most people first notice it between 16 and 24 weeks; sensations earlier than this are often caused by digestion or muscle movement.",
            "question": "Can you feel baby move at 12 weeks?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "A placenta at the front of the uterus can cushion early sensations, so movements may be noticed later or feel different. You should still learn your baby’s pattern and call immediately if it reduces or changes.",
            "question": "Does an anterior placenta delay movement?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "There is no set number that is normal for every baby, and routine counting is not recommended in UK guidance. Knowing your own baby’s usual pattern is more important. Follow any personalised plan from your maternity team.",
            "question": "Should I count kicks every day?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "No. The type of movement can change because there is less room, but you should continue to feel regular movement right up to and during labour.",
            "question": "Do babies move less near the due date?"
          }
        ],
