        "excerpt": "The symptoms that show up first, when a test becomes reliable, and what is worth doing in the days after a positive result.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "Some women notice tender breasts or fatigue from around week 4, shortly after implantation. Many notice nothing until week 6 or later, and some have almost no symptoms at all.",
            "question": "How early can pregnancy symptoms start?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Yes, if you test too early. hCG doubles every two to three days, so a test taken before your period is due may not detect it. Retest in three days if your period does not arrive.",
            "question": "Can you be pregnant and get a negative test?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Light spotting around the time your period was due can be implantation bleeding and is common. Heavy bleeding, or bleeding with pain, needs to be checked the same day.",
            "question": "Is spotting in early pregnancy normal?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": true,
        "heading": null,
        "heroImage": {
          "src": "https://cdn.sanity.io/images/hphu8zlu/production/a9f72a09f1f1be7b360d9ca77993d74d1a1a8edb-1672x941.webp?w=1600&fit=max&auto=format",
          "alt": "Early signs of pregnancy: what to look for and when",
          "width": 1672,
          "height": 941
        },
        "intro": "Early pregnancy rarely announces itself clearly. Some women notice a change within days of implantation; others feel completely normal until well past a missed period. Both are common, and neither says anything about how the pregnancy is going.",
        "locale": "en",
        "publishedAt": "2026-05-18",
        "relatedKeys": [
          "preparing-for-pregnancy",
          "baby-heartbeat-in-pregnancy"
        ],
        "reviewerId": "reviewer",
